 "result": "In this repo (package `tessel`, a Flask-style fork), the variables get into templates like this:\n\n**How `request`, `session` and `g` get there**\n- `src/tessel/templating.py:21` defines `_base_template_vars()`, the default context injector. It returns `{\"g\": ctx.g}` and, when a request is active, `{\"request\": ctx.request}`. It passes the concrete objects instead of the proxies.\n- `session` is not in that dict. It stays available as the global proxy, because replacing it would skip `session.accessed` tracking (see the comment at `templating.py:30`).\n- `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:184` pre-registers `_base_template_vars` in `template_ctx_injectors[None]`, so every app and bundle starts with it.\n- `App.fill_template_scope()` (`src/tessel/app.py:588`) runs all the injectors for the app and for the current request's bundles. It is called from the render functions at `templating.py:125` and `templating.py:167`. Values passed explicitly to `render_template` override anything the injectors return.\n\n**Adding your own variables**\n- Use the `@app.ctx_injector` decorator (`Scaffold.ctx_injector`, `src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:550`). Decorate a function that returns a dict, and its keys become template variables. Used on a bundle, it applies only to that bundle's views.\n- `@bundle.app_ctx_injector` (`src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:644`) registers a bundle's injector for every template in the app.",
